1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,520
Half off must be a deal, right?

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:10,080
But what if we told you it's not just about the number, it's about the story behind it.

3
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:16,200
Let's break down how ads, perception and pricing strategy play mind games.

4
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:20,160
And how smart companies adjust based on how we think.

5
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,480
Not all ads are equal.

6
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,480
Some promotions draw your eye right to the price.

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:32,280
Suddenly you're focused on cost above all else, and demand becomes more elastic.

8
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:35,760
That means if the price isn't right, people bounce.

9
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:41,080
So when an ad emphasizes price, the price better be good.

10
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:47,000
And in this case, when you become more elastic, you want to lower the price on that good.

11
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:49,240
But some ads go deeper.

12
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,680
They tell stories, they stir emotion.

13
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:57,360
These ads don't just grab your attention, they increase your willingness to pay.

14
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:02,640
You become less price sensitive, and that is inelastic demand.

15
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:09,120
When done right, you can charge more, raise prices, and people feel good about it.

16
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,080
But there's a catch.

17
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:17,040
Promote too much and customers start waiting for the next sell.  Full price.

18
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:18,240
No thanks.

19
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:19,400
They've seen better.

20
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:23,080
You've trained your shoppers to see regular price as a rip off.

21
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:27,520
And once that perception sets in, it's hard to win them back.

22
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:30,880
Want to change how people feel about price?

23
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:32,480
Reframe it.

24
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:35,560
Let's say you're buying a pair of premium headphones.

25
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:39,760
The tax says $150, okay?

26
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:43,400
Now imagine it says you save $50 instead.

27
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:48,320
Same price, totally different reaction.  Why?

28
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:50,600
Because shoppers don't calculate.

29
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:51,760
They compare.

30
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:57,440
Framing a deal as a gain taps into the psychology of wins.

31
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:00,760
And people love feeling like they got the upper hand.

32
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:04,600
Behavioral economist Richard Tyler breaks it down.

33
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:08,280
Acquisition utility is what the product's worth to you.

34
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:12,920
Transaction utility is how the deal feels.

35
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:18,760
Promotion might not change the product, but they massively boost the second kind of happiness.

36
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:33,760
So, yeah, promos sell products, but more than that, they shape perception, understand elasticity, frame your value, design your pricing to speak to how people buy, not just what they buy.

37
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:35,360
That's the real promo power.